WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:06.880
If you’ve ever visited a natural history museum,&nbsp;
you’ve probably seen timelines like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:12.800
or maybe branching ones like this, showing you&nbsp;
the evolutionary chronology of familiar mammal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:18.080
species. And in most of these timelines, if you&nbsp;
all the way back to the beginning, you’ll find a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:24.720
pint-sized version of mammals you know today. This&nbsp;
isn’t a coincidence. Modern mammals originated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:30.880
from species tiny enough outlast the extinction&nbsp;
event which wiped out the dinosaurs, and then grew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:37.280
in size and diversity to fill the ecological&nbsp;
niches the dinosaurs left in their wake. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:42.560
result of this shared origin, looking at the&nbsp;
‘first drafts’ of mammals, so to speak, kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:47.840
feels like looking at someone’s baby photos. And&nbsp;
most of these miniature ancestors of the species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:54.320
we know today emerged during the Eocene period,&nbsp;
a geological epoch which gets its name from Greek&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.920
terms meaning “New Dawn,” an appropriate moniker&nbsp;
considering the Eocene represented the ‘dawn’ of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:04.960
modern mammals. So, let’s take a look at the tiny&nbsp;
mammals who populated this period, and better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:09.680
understand their connection to the mammals&nbsp;
we know today. Beginning with Moeritherium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:16.000
this extinct genus of rotund, stubby Eocene&nbsp;
mammals is a distant relation of mammoths and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:22.240
elephants. The size and body shape of the animal&nbsp;
makes the Moeritherium look more like a modern pig&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:28.160
than an elephant, standing only 70 centimeters or&nbsp;
2.3 feet high at the shoulder. The shape of their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.720
skulls suggests they didn’t have a fully-formed&nbsp;
trunk like an modern elephant, but more of a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:38.080
stumpy upper lip for grasping aquatic vegetation.&nbsp;
Although maybe an offshoot instead the direct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:43.120
ancestor to modern elephants, it’s still amusing&nbsp;
to think that other species in this diminutive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:49.520
taxonomic order would one day become the largest&nbsp;
land animals on the planet. Here’s another genus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.800
from the early Eocene which looks quite different&nbsp;
from it’s gigantic modern ancestors. This is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.800 --> 00:02:01.440
Ambulocetus the four limbed ancestor of the modern&nbsp;
whale. Their name means "walking whales” because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.320
scientists once belied they were amphibious and&nbsp;
could walk on land as well as swim. Although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:11.360
most modern research suggests they were fully&nbsp;
aquatic, the concept of a sharp-toothed, furry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:16.480
whale with legs is still pretty incredible. Most&nbsp;
modern whale species actually have trace remnants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:21.920
of vestigial leg bones in their tails however&nbsp;
from this early land to ocean transition period:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.960
pretty interesting. What’s also interesting is&nbsp;
Ambulocetus, as a predator, might have preyed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:32.640
on some early genus of horses due to a temporal&nbsp;
overlap. If this is indeed the case, the idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:39.200
a whale which ate horses is… quite jarring,&nbsp;
to say the least. Speaking of early horses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:44.800
Hyracotherium is an extinct genus of very small&nbsp;
odd-toed ungulates basal to the Equid family, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:50.160
taxonomic classification which eventually gave us&nbsp;
the horse — as well as the donkey and the zebra.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:55.360
Although not technically Equid themselves like&nbsp;
they were once believed, Hyracotherium have quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:03:00.320
the evolutionary lineage, which is impressive&nbsp;
considering just how tiny these species were.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.320 --> 00:03:06.720
These proto-horses maxed out at about 9 kg or 20&nbsp;
pounds, about the weight of a Miniature Schnauzer.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.560
Taking to the skies we have the Onychonycteris,&nbsp;
a creature from the early Eocene which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:18.640
looks somewhat alarming from fossils alone, but&nbsp;
more familiar in re creations. — it’s the bat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.640 --> 00:03:23.120
or the most primitive taxonomic ancestor&nbsp;
of the modern bats we’re familiar with.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:27.360
Analysis of the bone structure suggests&nbsp;
the Onychonycteris could already fly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:33.840
and probably echo-locate. Indeed, compared to&nbsp;
other species, it seems. early bats already had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:41.200
it all figured out… evolutionary speaking. Up next&nbsp;
is a middle Eocene animal I felt I had to mention.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:46.240
The Andrewsarchus is an extinct genus which&nbsp;
might have been the largest predatory mammal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:51.600
ever to walk the earth. The three foot-long wolf&nbsp;
like skull we’ve uncovered alone suggests an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.560
animal even larger than a modern grizzly bear,&nbsp;
for example. So, what is this early mammal the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:02.160
ancestor of? Well, not much, actually. Most&nbsp;
scientists think the Andrewsarchus has no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.680
direct evolutionary descendants whatsoever,&nbsp;
and was an offshoot which died out, proving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:12.800
in biology as in life, you can never tell what&nbsp;
who will succeed long-term based on early signs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:19.120
of promise. Ending our adventure through first&nbsp;
drafts of mammals, we have the Omomyidae, a group&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:24.960
of early primates with massive eyes, similar to&nbsp;
the startling peepers on a modern-day Tarsier.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.760 --> 00:04:32.720
So what are these perpetually shocked animals&nbsp;
the ancestor of? You and I. Well, maybe. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:38.320
difficult to say for certain if Omomyidae are the&nbsp;
evolutionary ancestors of humans and other apes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:45.360
but it’s certainly possible. If so, our&nbsp;
ancestors weighed around .5 kilograms, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:51.360
just over one pound. Pretty tiny. Despite this,&nbsp;
they had some early versions of features your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:57.200
might recognize on yourself, like grasping hands&nbsp;
with complex digits. It’s difficult to know much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:02.400
else about these organism dye to scattered&nbsp;
remains, but if they are an early branch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:08.080
in our familial tree you might be looking at your&nbsp;
evolutionary baby photos, to put it a certain way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.120 --> 00:05:14.080
And so, our journey through early Eocene versions&nbsp;
of mammals you might recognize today comes to an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:18.160
end. I hope you enjoyed this video, and&nbsp;
learned a thing or two along the way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

